# id: M62-lock-expiry-off-by-one
# covering-test: TestTheDeadlineIsComparedAgainstTheReadTimestamp
# package: ./kv/
# expect: killed
# power-seeds: 200
# power-floor: 9
# power-ceiling: 80
# power-measured: 18 of 200, first at seed 20, under current at A6's close with resolution-only-breaks-expired-locks in the harness. The clean tree at the same 200 seeds is 0 detections and sweepfail=0. BEFORE the detector it measured 0 of 300 and then 0 of 100 with the sweep detector on, and that pair of zeros was the FINDING rather than the instrument: 33 census fields moved and TxnLostToResolver went 0 -> 2, so the defect was happening and no oracle and no exit criterion said anything, because aborting a transaction is a legal outcome and every client-facing checker is blind to it by construction (DESIGN-A6 sections 13.4, 35.4). Floored at half the measured rate with the ceiling at four times the first detecting seed, which is the same margin M14 and M45 carry. The rate is set by how often a resolver reaches a primary while the transaction is genuinely live AND undecided -- at seed 0 five of six rolled-back records are the coordinator's own abort -- so it is a narrow window rather than a weak oracle (DESIGN-A6 section 40)
# mutates: a lock is never respected as live -- the expiry test always falls through to roll-back. SYMMETRIC: every replica breaks the same live locks, so the cluster agrees and a transaction is aborted by a competitor that had no right to abort it. Atomicity survives, because a rolled-back transaction is a legitimate outcome; what is lost is that a live coordinator's work is destroyed under it
--- a/kv/resolve.go
+++ b/kv/resolve.go
@@ -87,7 +87,7 @@
 		return ResolveForward, rec.CommitTS, nil
 	case ok:
 		return ResolveBack, hlc.Timestamp{}, nil
-	case readTS.LessEq(l.Deadline):
+	case readTS.Less(l.Deadline) && false:
 		return ResolveWait, hlc.Timestamp{}, nil
 	default:
 		return ResolveBack, hlc.Timestamp{}, nil
